column2:
You were admitted with disseminated herpes zoster affecting various parts of your body including your face, arms, trunk, abdomen, lower extremities, and even your eyes. This condition caused significant discomfort and pain throughout your stay here.

During your hospitalization, we performed several tests and interventions aimed at managing your symptoms and preventing complications associated with your condition. These included lumbar punctures, cerebrospinal fluid analysis, imaging studies, blood workups, urine analyses, and administration of intravenous medications such as acyclovir.

As part of our treatment plan, we have prescribed certain medications for you upon discharge. Please adhere strictly to these prescriptions:

Gabapentin 600mg orally thrice daily; 
Simvastatin 10mg orally nightly;
Acetaminophen 1000mg orally thrice daily; 
Polyethylene Glycol 17 grams orally each night before bedtime; 
Compression Stockings (below-knee type), using one pair per day.



We recommend that you wear compression stockings below the knees daily for optimal benefits. Additionally, if you experience any severe side effects from your medications, do not hesitate to contact us immediately.



Our medical staff has been informed about your case, so they may visit you at home periodically to monitor your recovery and adjust your treatment plans accordingly.



For future reference, kindly review the attached documents containing details regarding your current prescription regimen and upcoming appointment schedules.



If you encounter any issues related to your health or require clarification on anything mentioned herein, don't hesitate to reach out to us directly via phone at _______________________ .

Dear Mr., 

You were hospitalized due to worsening symptoms related to your multiple sclerosis. During your stay, we conducted various tests including imaging studies of your brain and neck. Your neurological examination remained relatively consistent throughout your hospitalization. However, some notable changes included decreased sensation in your left arm and legs to light touch and temperature.

Hospital Treatments or Procedures:
During your hospitalization, you underwent several medical evaluations and received supportive treatment for managing your symptoms.

Discharge Medications:
Continue taking the following medications exactly as directed:
Albuterol inhaler as needed for breathing issues;
baclofen orally four times a day for muscle stiffness;
Diazepam orally six times a day for anxiety relief;
docu-sodium orally twice a day to help manage stool regularity;
Duloxetine orally once a day for depression management;
Oxycarbamazepine orally two times a day for seizures prevention;
Senna orally twice a day for digestive health support;
Verapamil orally twenty-four hours apart for blood pressure regulation;
Meclizine orally twice a day for motion sickness prevention;
Hydrocodone-acetaminophen orally four times a day as needed for pain management;
Magnesium oxide orally once a day to minimize gastrointestinal side effects;

Important Self-Care Instructions:
Take your prescribed medication regularly and do not stop them abruptly unless advised by your healthcare provider. Monitor your body temperature closely since there might be potential risks associated with certain conditions affecting thermoregulation. Avoid heavy lifting until cleared by your physician. If experiencing persistent confusion or disorientation seek immediate attention.



=============================================

Based solely on the discharge report, here are the generated Discharge Instructions:

Take your prescribed medications exactly as instructed:
Acetaminophen 1000mg orally every 8 hours for moderate pain relief;
OxyCodone (Immediate Release) 5mg orally every 6 hours for severe pain relief, but avoid taking it if you're feeling sleepy;
Albuterol Inhaler 2 puffs inhale every 4 hours for shortness of breath relief;
Amitriptyline 20mg orally once daily for depression symptoms;
Bisacodyl 10mg rectally once daily for second-line constipation prevention;
Cetirizine 10mg orally once daily for allergic reactions;
EPINEPHrine (EpiPen) 0.3mg inject once monthly for emergency situations;
Escitalopram Oxalate 10mg orally once daily for depression symptoms;
Fexofenadine 180mg orally once daily for allergies;
Fluticasone Propionate Nasal Spray 2 sprays nostrils twice daily for nasal congestion;
Methocarbamol 750mg orally once daily for muscle relaxation;
mometasone 200mcg actuation inhalation twice daily for respiratory issues;
Pantoprazole 40mg orally twice daily for stomach acid reduction;
Sumatriptan Succinate 6mg subcutaneously once daily for migraines;
Zolpidem Tartrate 10mg orally once daily for insomnia.

Monitor your condition closely and seek immediate medical attention if you experience:
severe abdominal pain or difficulty swallowing;
shortness of breath or chest tightness;
increased urination frequency or sudden loss of bladder control;
numbness or tingling sensations in hands or feet;

Maintain good hydration by drinking plenty of water throughout the day.

Clearance to resume driving and other activities will be discussed during your upcoming appointment.

Note: Please consult your healthcare provider regarding any questions or concerns about these instructions.
